    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Few places in the ocean benefit from long-term multidecadal-to-centennial studies of biodiversity and of the processes that may regulate diversity patterns. Monterey Bay is one such place with a rich legacy of quantitative, semi-quantitative, and qualitative work exceeding 150 years. We seek — for the first time — to assemble this legacy in three ways: 1) Oral histories of those familiar with Monterey Bay biodiversity over the past 50 years, 2) a database that captures results over three eras (1860s-1920s; 1930s-1980s; 1990s-2016) while focusing on quantitative research of communities that has been repeated once or multiple times, or would be highly amenable to repetition (database available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/jc051wf2528), and 3) establishing long-term sampling of the intertidal zone around Hopkins Marine Station (analyzing and continuing to sample the Hewatt-Sagarin intertidal transect). \r\n\r\nOver the last year (2016-2017) we have interviewed 20 researchers and students who visited Hopkins between 1940 and 1980. These oral histories include inquiries into their memories of abundant species in the intertidal and kelp forests, drivers of change to those invertebrate and algae communities, and memories of the scientists they worked with during their tenure at Hopkins. Our goal is to compare and summarize these memories, and pair the results with long-term data analysis, to create a more complete understanding of historic biodiversity and enhance our understanding of how and why these systems are in flux.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Pearse, J,  Pearse, V, Thomas, T , Mach, M, Baxter, C, Carlton, J, Elahi, R, Micheli, F, and Watanabe, J. (2017). Oral histories of researchers, students and visitors recalling biodiversity changes in the intertidal and shallow-water of Monterey Bay, California. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/mv006wz3348",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "displayLabel": "Preferred Citation",
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
